A||||5||Pretender||front left hub assembly on '88 gt(wheel bearings)||||5
Z||000000||Pretender||09-14-1999||02:56 AM||||My left front hub assembly has play in it, i'm guessing that the wheel bearings are in there somewhere and one might have spun??, i noticed the bolts with the star in them behind the plate(lug bolts are atttached to) Is this how to access them?.  How do i fix the problem?. will i need a hub puller? any other special tools? all info on this subject is greatly appreciated!<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||ChadMan||09-14-1999||09:58 AM||||Replacing the front wheel bearings isn't a bad job - I had to replace my outer front bearings and repack the inners on the 86 SE last fall.  No special tools are needed (though a torque wrench is very helpful) - just remove the caliper and hang it safely out of the way, remove the cotter pin and nut from the hub, then grab the rotor by hand and tug on it until it comes free.  The outer bearing just falls out, and while you're in there, you should replace the bearing race too - I drove mine out with a hammer handle.  You'll have to pry out the grease seal to access the inner bearing.  The bearings, races and grease seals are dirt cheap, even at NAPA.<P>Two cautions:  Be very careful and gentle when seating the new race and seals. Tap gently and take your time.  And when reinstalling the rotor and bearings, you'll walk a fine line between overtightening and undertightening (here's where the torque wrench comes in).  I did my job without a torque wrench, and a week later, I had to re-tighten the nut after the new bearings seated themselves.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pretender||09-14-1999||01:48 PM||||my rotors aren't held on by a nut/ cotterpin. all i have to do to remove them is to remove the caliper. Were your wheel bearings inside the rotor? mine must be inside the hub assembly, i think. ||||reg||5
Z||000003||geezer||09-14-1999||08:38 PM||||Front wheel bearings on '88 are a sealed unit.  You can't adjust or lube; all you get to do is replace!!  Unfortuneately, not cheap.  Remove the caliper, (2 torx or star<BR>bolts) then there are 3 same size torx accessed through a hole as you rotate the wheel hub to line them up.  I've done 2 '88's and had some trouble getting the unit out as it tends to corrode/rust.  Lots of "Blaster",<BR>some "friendly" taps and some prying and I eventually got them.  Couldn't get replacements locally; got them through Fiero Store.  Hope this helps. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChadMan||09-15-1999||09:11 AM||||Lucky me, I've yet to have to tinker with the wheel bearings on my 88.  I guess my tips will be really helpful if/when you buy an 84-87...||||reg||10
